  "body": "`store` in `src/tide_cache.py` keeps every tide reading for ever. Readings should expire after a retention period, but how long the harbour keeps a reading is a policy call the repository does not state. Expire stale readings once the period is known.\n\n**Delivers:** Stale tide readings expire. `store` (src/tide_cache.py:4) records when each reading was stored and, on every call, removes from `READINGS` (src/tide_cache.py:1) any reading stored more than 7 days earlier, with the period held in a named module constant. `store` is the module's only function and a reading is an opaque value (src/tide_cache.py:4-5), so the stored-at time is the only age available and `store` is the only place to apply expiry. The 7 days is an operator-stated constraint: asked \"How many days should a tide reading be kept before it expires?\", the operator answered \"Keep readings for 7 days.\"\n\n**Test contract:** With the clock controlled by the test, so no test sleeps:\n\n- A reading stored 6 days ago is still held after a later `store` call.\n- A reading stored more than 7 days ago is gone after a later `store` call.\n- A reading stored exactly 7 days ago is still held: expiry starts once the age exceeds the period.\n- The reading passed to `store` is always held, and surviving readings keep their storage order.\n\n**Files:** `src/tide_cache.py` (modified), `tests/test_tide_cache.py` (created; the repository has no tests yet)\n\n## Flow-out\n\n- [ ] Touched area/subsystem profiles updated with what changed\n- [ ] Prover-validated assumptions captured as session candidates (durable at flush)\n- [ ] New decisions / lessons / follow-ups surfaced during the build recorded\n",
